{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/refugee",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "refugee"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The forced life situation and legal status of a person forced to leave their country of origin due to a well-founded fear of persecution, armed conflict, violence, or other events seriously threatening their life, health, and freedom."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "According to the Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees, 1951, a refugee is a person outside their country who is unable or unwilling to return there due to a well-founded fear of persecution for five specific reasons: race, religion, nationality, membership in a particular social group, or political opinion. Legally and sociologically, refugee status is characterised by three main elements: forced migration, crossing a state border (individuals fleeing to safer regions of their country are called internally displaced persons (IDPs), and loss of protection from their home government. Refugee status is associated with special international protection, including the fundamental principle of non-refoulement, which prohibits the return of such individuals to places where they would face danger."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "3.15"
  ],
  "broader": {
    "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3",
    "prefLabel": {
      "en": "Culture, migration, poverty, and society"
    }
  },
